src/test/stress/REGRESSIONS

Historical bugs this suite is meant to catch
============================================

A stress suite that has never been shown to fail is a suite that proves
nothing.  This file is the list of real, already-fixed bugs in the
CONCURRENTLY commands, taken from the git history, together with the
scenario that ought to catch each one and the result of actually
checking that -- by putting the bug back and running the suite.

Every entry starts at "untested".  An entry is only moved to "catches"
once the reverted tree has been run and failed, with the failure rate
recorded; an entry that survives a long run is marked "MISSED", which is
a gap in the suite, not a footnote.

The list was built by grepping the history for the commands this suite
drives, so it is biased towards bugs someone wrote a commit message
about.  It is not a coverage target: a suite that catches all of these
and nothing else is still only as good as the last bug someone found.

How to test one
===============

  git switch -c revert-<commit> <base>
  git revert --no-commit <commit>        # or hand-reintroduce, see below
  ninja -C build && meson test -C build --suite setup --suite stress

Reverting the fix alone is usually not enough to see a failure at the
default duration -- these are races.  Run the named scenario in a loop
at a higher stressval, and record how often it fails:

  for i in $(seq 20); do
    meson test -C build --suite setup --suite stress <scenario> \
      --test-args ... PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_concurrently=4'
  done

Two practical notes.  Most fixes older than about 2024 no longer revert
cleanly -- the surrounding code has moved -- and have to be
reintroduced by hand; the entry says what the essential change is.  And
several of these bugs are only observable in an assertion build, which
is what the suite should be run under anyway.

A note on what "caught" means
=============================

Three of the checks do the real work here, and it is worth knowing
which one is expected to fire, because that determines what the
scenario has to declare:

  amcheck        bt_index_parent_check(heapallindexed => true) at the
                 end of the run.  This is what finds heap rows missing
                 from an index.  It checks the indexes the scenario
                 declared, and the primary key of each of its tables.
  index_vs_seq   an indexed read and a seqscan of the same snapshot must
                 agree.  Requires the btree_abalance index.
  the invariant  balances, ledger_sum and friends: a wrong answer that
                 the workload itself can observe.

An entry whose scenario declares no btree index is not being checked by
amcheck at all, whatever else it runs.  See "Where the suite falls
short" at the end.

The detector has a history of its own worth knowing about.  Until
1f28982e409, "amcheck: Fix snapshot usage in bt_index_parent_check",
that function fingerprinted the index under SnapshotAny while the index
had been built under an MVCC snapshot, so an index built by CIC could
be reported as missing heap tuples that it was correct to omit.  Since
that is exactly the configuration this suite uses, against a base that
predates it every amcheck failure here is suspect.  Check for that
commit before believing one.


Wrong results: heap rows missing from a concurrently built index
================================================================

This is the class the suite exists for, and the one amcheck's
heapallindexed catches directly.

  fdd965d074d  2021-10-23  Avoid race in RelationBuildDesc() affecting
                           CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY.
      A backend that absorbs a relevant invalidation in the middle of
      RelationBuildDesc() on the new index does not see the catalog
      change by its next transaction start, so it stops maintaining the
      index; queries using it then silently miss rows.
      expect: index_build_churn.
      revert: by hand -- disable the "goto retry" at the end of
              RelationBuildDesc().  That is the whole of the behaviour;
              the in_progress_list bookkeeping can stay.
      also:   already guarded upstream by contrib/amcheck/t/002_cic.pl,
              which shipped with this commit and is the direct ancestor
              of this suite's design: pgbench, an advisory lock so only
              one CIC runs at a time, and bt_index_check(heapallindexed)
              from inside the pgbench script.  Reverting is therefore a
              calibration exercise -- does this suite catch it too, and
              how much faster -- rather than a gap being closed.
      status: CATCHES, but only with chaos -- see below.  1 failure in 2
              runs at stressval 8 with the relcache_probe profile, and
              originally 2 in 8 without it, as

                ERROR:  heap tuple (0,94) from table "pgbench_history"
                lacks matching index tuple within index
                "pgb_history_delta_idx"

              and 0 in 6 with the fix restored.  Nothing at the default
              duration, and nothing at all -- 0 in 15 -- from
              repack_dml_s1, hot_prune or unique_and_gapless, which is
              why index_build_churn exists.

              What it needed was rate, not a different mechanism.  The
              scenarios above spread CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY over
              several rotation entries and several tables, so any one
              index is rebuilt seldom; 002_cic spends about a third of
              its transactions on a build-and-check of one index on a
              tiny table.  index_build_churn copies that: one table, one
              declared index, a rotation containing nothing but
              drop_create_index, and 30 clients inserting.  Raising the
              client count from 10 to 30 took it from 1 in 6 to 2 in 8.

              Calibration answer, since this entry was listed as one:
              002_cic catches the same reintroduction 2 runs in 2, at
              the default duration, in a few seconds.  It is strictly
              better than this suite here and must not be retired on the
              strength of index_build_churn.  See the note in
              "Duplicated upstream tests".

      the catch lapsed, and chaos brought it back: this is the one entry
              of the twenty-four where building the revert pairs found
              something.  With the retry disabled the scenario failed 0
              times in 22 runs at stressval 8, where 2 in 8 would make 22
              clean runs a one-in-six-hundred coincidence.

              The revert was not at fault, and that was measured rather
              than assumed.  A probe where the "goto retry" used to be
              fires 181 times in a single run -- 145 times for
              pgbench_history, whose index list is the thing that has to
              go stale, and 36 for pgb_history_delta_idx itself.  So the
              window was still being entered constantly and the
              invalidation still ignored, exactly as before the fix, and
              amcheck still passed.  What had changed was not whether the
              race is reached but how long the stale entry survives:
              something absorbs an invalidation and heals it before
              anything reads the index list.

              Widening that window is what brought it back.  The scenario
              now declares the relcache_probe chaos profile, which jitters
              relcache-build-catalogs-read -- where the entry has been
              built and the invalidation is about to be checked -- and
              accept-invalidation-messages, which is how long a backend
              goes on using what it has.

              The rate with it is unstable and worth knowing about before
              relying on this one: 1 failure in 2 runs when the profile
              was first measured, and 1 in 12 when the same commit was
              re-measured later on a busier machine.  Both were at
              stressval 8 with the profile firing hard -- over a hundred
              thousand sleeps a run.  Budget a dozen runs, not two.

              This is the clearest argument for the chaos machinery in the
              file.  A catch does not lapse loudly: the scenario went on
              passing, and would have gone on passing, while no longer
              reaching the thing it was written for.  The pairs are what
              made that visible, and the profile is what fixed it.

