Scans that trust the visibility map for TIDs a vacuum has taken away
====================================================================

A scan holds a TID it read earlier and asks the visibility map whether
it still needs to look at the heap.  A vacuum that has removed the TID
and marked its page all-visible in the meantime turns that question into
the wrong answer, and the scan returns a row that is not there.

  459e7bf8e2f  2025-04-02  Remove HeapBitmapScan's skip_fetch
                           optimization
      A bitmap heap scan needing no columns from the table could skip
      reading an all-visible page and take its tuple count from the
      bitmap.  The bitmap is older than the page, so a vacuum running
      since could have removed the dead TIDs it refers to, and the scan
      counted them.
      expect: bitmap_skip_fetch, whose invariant is inside the workload
              because it needs one snapshot: the same count taken by a
              bitmap heap scan and by a sequential scan inside one
              repeatable-read transaction.  count(*) with an indexable
              qual and nothing else is what makes the scan node's qual
              and target list both empty, which is the test the
              optimization used to decide it could skip.
      revert: git revert conflicts -- the bitmap heap scan has been
              rebuilt around the read stream API since, and
              table_beginscan_bm() now takes a flags word rather than a
              bool.  Hand-resolved: SO_NEED_TUPLES comes back as another
              ScanOptions flag rather than as the old parameter, the two
              fields go back on BitmapHeapScanDescData (the base
              HeapScanDescData has acquired an rs_vmbuffer of its own
              since, for heap_page_prune_opt, and the two are managed
              separately), and nodeBitmapHeapscan.c sets the flag from
              the same qual/targetlist test it used to.
      status: CATCHES, 1 failure in 1 run at stress_seconds=20, as

                ERROR:  bitmap heap scan counted 4097 rows, the table
                has 4000 under the same snapshot

              and 3 runs in 3 are clean with the revert taken back out,
              which is what says the scenario is watching this and not
              something else.  4000 wide rows at fillfactor 10 put about
              one row on a page, so the scan is long enough for a vacuum
              to get ahead of it -- with the 200-row tables the
              index-only scenario uses, the scan finishes before a
              vacuum can overtake it and nothing is seen.

  The same shape, still open, is the GiST and SP-GiST index-only scan
  bug recorded further down: a scan queues TIDs, lets the page go, and
  the index-only path then trusts the visibility map for them.  459e7bf8
  is the case that was closed by removing the optimization rather than
  by building the interlock.

ON CONFLICT arbiter selection during concurrent index operations
================================================================

Four fixes, all recent, all in the same area: which indexes a
speculative insertion is allowed to use while an index is being built
or swapped.  The failure mode is a spurious "duplicate key value
violates unique constraint" or a deadlock -- which pgbench turns into an
aborted client, so the suite fails loudly if it hits it.

  bc32a12e0db  2025-11-24  Fix infer_arbiter_index during concurrent
                           index operations
      Only indisvalid indexes were considered, so two transactions could
      infer different arbiter sets during CIC/RIC.
      expect: unique_and_gapless.
      revert: git revert conflicts (plancat.c has moved, and this tree
              changes it too).  Hand-applied instead, which is two lines
              in infer_arbiter_indexes(): skip !indisvalid rather than
              !indisready, and drop the "at least one valid" test at the
              end.  The injection points and specs the commit shipped
              are not needed to put the bug back.
      status: CATCHES, 4 failures in 8 runs at stressval 4, as

                ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint
                "pgbench_accounts_pkey_ccnew"

              and none in 8 at the default duration, or at stressval 4
              before the tuning below.  8 runs with the fix restored are
              clean.

              Two things had to change first, and the second is the
              interesting one:

              - Nothing swapped the index the upserts actually resolve
                to.  Every arbiter here is pgbench_accounts_pkey, and
                the standard rotation reaches a primary key only through
                reindex_table_concurrently, as one index among the
                table's.  unique_and_gapless now runs
                reindex_pkey_concurrently as well.
              - No upsert in the suite ever inserted.  upsert_merge's
                own comment says it: the row always exists, so all three
                of its modes take the matched path.  That means
                speculative insertion -- the thing an inconsistent
                arbiter set breaks -- was never reached at all.  The new
                upsert_contend load keeps 16 keys above pgbench's range
                appearing and disappearing, so several clients race to
                insert the same absent key.

              Both were confirmed against the deterministic gate for
              this bug, test_misc/010_index_concurrently_upsert, which
              fails immediately with the same reintroduction: that is
              what said the hand-application was right and the scenario,
              not the revert, was what needed work.

  2bc7e886fc1  2025-12-01  Fix ON CONFLICT ON CONSTRAINT during REINDEX
                           CONCURRENTLY
      During the swap, several indexes match the constraint's
      definition; only the named one was considered.
      expect: unique_and_gapless -- upsert_contend names the constraint
              in one of its modes, on a key that may not be there yet.
      revert: by hand: in infer_arbiter_indexes(), make the branch for
              "some other index, and a constraint was named" skip the
              index instead of matching it on the constraint index's
              attributes.  That is the whole of the behaviour the commit
              changed; the rest of it is a refactor.
      status: CATCHES, 4 failures in 8 runs at stressval 8, as

                ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint
                "pgbench_accounts_pkey_ccnew"

              and none in 8 at stressval 4.  6 runs with the fix
              restored are clean at stressval 8.

              It needs twice the stress of bc32a12e0db above because the
              window is narrower.  There, the arbiter sets disagree for
              as long as the new index is indisready and not yet valid,
              which is the whole build; here they disagree only across
              the instant the constraint's conindid is swapped.

  90eae926abb  2025-12-02  Fix ON CONFLICT with REINDEX CONCURRENTLY and
                           partitions
      Arbiters for each partition are derived from the parent's, so a
      partition's auxiliary index built by RIC was not considered.
      expect: partitions -- the partition_upsert load routes an upsert
              through the parent while the rotation rebuilds a
              partition's index.
      revert: by hand, and it is one line: the commit added the whole
              "unparented indexes may be arbiters too" fallback to
              ExecInitPartitionInfo, so disabling that block puts the
              bug back.  additional_arbiters then stays 0 and the count
              check still balances, which is what makes the failure a
              wrong answer rather than an internal error.
      status: CATCHES, but thinly: 1 failure in 6 runs at stressval 4
              and 1 in 8 at stressval 8, as

                ERROR:  duplicate key value violates unique constraint
                "pgb_part_1_pkey_ccnew"

              on the transient index a rebuild leaves behind.  The rate
              does not improve with stressval because what gates it is
              how often the rotation rebuilds a partition's index while
              a contending insert lands on that partition.

              As with bc32a12e0db, the scenario could not reach this at
              all until it had an upsert that actually inserts:
              partition_upsert only ever meets rows that already exist.
              The new partition_upsert_contend load races several
              clients to insert the same absent key through the parent.
              It is confined to a sixteen-id contention band whose rows
              carry no value, so deleting and re-inserting them does not
              move the sum partition_sum watches -- and that check only
              asserts when every row is present anyway.  partition_dml
              and partition_upsert were moved off the band so nothing
              else writes value into it.

              Distinguish this from the residual below by signature: a
              duplicate key error is this entry, "invalid arbiter index
              list" is not.

