Fixes in this branch that nothing here guards
=============================================

One of the four server fixes committed alongside this suite is not
demonstrated by it.  The RI one was not either, until the scenario was
rebuilt around what the race actually needs; that is written up here
because the same reasoning applies to the one still outstanding.

  ri_triggers.c, the RI fast path -- now guarded
      The window is between reading conindid out of the cached
      RI_ConstraintInfo and opening that index, with
      table_open(pk_relid, RowShareLock) in between -- and it is that
      table_open which processes the invalidation a concurrent REINDEX
      CONCURRENTLY swap produced.  A few instructions wide, opening once
      per constraint per trigger-firing batch.

      Reaching it took two changes, both measured rather than reasoned:

        The parent has to be small AND quiet.  The race is against its
        primary key being swapped, so the rebuild must finish quickly.
        pgbench_accounts is quiet but has a hundred thousand rows and
        reproduced nothing in fifty-odd runs.  pgbench_tellers has ten
        rows but is what every TPC-B transaction updates, so a rebuild
        of its key waits for lockers instead of finishing: nothing in
        eight runs.  A thousand-row table nothing else writes works.

        The primary key has to be rebuilt on its own.  Until
        reindex_pkey_concurrently was added, the only command that
        touched one was reindex_table_concurrently, which rebuilds every
        index of the table and so swaps the key at a fraction of the
        rate.

      With both: three failures in twenty runs at stressval 4, none at
      the default duration, and none in ten with the fix restored.

      One dead end worth not repeating: batching many checks into one
      statement makes it worse, not better.  The index is opened once
      per batch, so batching lowers the rate of the thing being raced.

  plancat.c, get_relation_info()
      The window is between RelationGetIndexList() handing back an
      index list and index_open() on one of its members, on a standby,
      with replay of the drop landing in between.  Also a few
      instructions.

The planner one is still unguarded, and the RI experience says what to
try: raise the rate of the thing being raced rather than the rate of
the workload.  Here that means a standby replaying drops of an index on
a small, quiet relation, planned against repeatedly -- not
drop_create_index over a busy hundred-thousand-row table.  Failing
that, it is what injection points are for: stop the backend between
RelationGetIndexList() and index_open(), let replay commit, release.
That is how upstream tested 5bbdfa8a18d.  Until one or the other, it is
committed on the argument -- which is about lock coverage and is
checkable by reading -- rather than on a demonstration.

