      directories afterwards shows why: the publisher holds the single
      scale-1 branch row, the subscriber's pgbench_branches is empty,
      and pg_subscription_rel says srsubstate='r'.  The subscriber is
      permanently short a table and the catalog considers it fully
      synchronized.  Both failing runs show it.

      The publisher log pins the cause.  The branch tablesync worker
      started at 07:36:14.966, REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) pgbench_branches
      committed at 07:36:14.997, the worker logged its first conflict at
      07:36:16.010 and reported "has finished" 67ms later.  The rewrite
      landed in the window between the worker exporting its snapshot and
      issuing its COPY.

      That the COPY sees nothing is documented and not a bug: a rewrite
      is not MVCC-safe, so it "will appear empty to concurrent
      transactions, if they are using a snapshot taken before the
      command committed" (mvcc-caveats).  The caveat bounds the damage by
      arguing that a reader would hold ACCESS SHARE and block the
      rewrite -- which is exactly what a tablesync worker does not do
      between exporting its snapshot and starting to read.  So the
      bounded, transient anomaly the caveat describes becomes an
      unbounded one here: the worker copies zero rows, marks the table
      ready, and the apply worker spends the rest of the subscription's
      life logging update_missing against rows that will never exist.

      What makes it silent rather than merely wrong is that nothing in
      the stream repairs it.  TRUNCATE is not MVCC-safe either, but
      TRUNCATE is replicated, so a tablesync that copies an empty table
      still converges.  A rewrite emits no replication message at all.

      Reproduce, on two clean clusters, 6 runs in 6 -- the subscriber
      ends with 0 of 10000 rows and srsubstate='r':

        publisher : CREATE TABLE t(i int primary key);
                    INSERT INTO t SELECT g FROM generate_series(1,10000) g;
                    CREATE PUBLICATION p FOR TABLE t;
        subscriber: CREATE TABLE t(i int primary key);
                    -- started with debug_discard_caches=1, which widens
                    -- the pre-COPY window; the stress runs above hit it
                    -- under ordinary load without any such help
        publisher : REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) t;   -- in a loop, while
        subscriber: CREATE SUBSCRIPTION s ... PUBLICATION p;

      Not specific to REPACK, and not new with it: substituting

        ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN i TYPE bigint;

      for the REPACK loses the rows the same way, twice in two.  Any
      rewriting form will do, which dates the exposure to logical
      replication itself rather than to REPACK.  REPACK only makes it
      easier to reach, being the one such command that does not hold
      AccessExclusiveLock for its whole run.

      Not yet established: whether this is known and accepted upstream.
      The logical replication documentation says nothing about it,
      tablesync.c carries no comment about it, and the mvcc-caveats
      reasoning quoted above does not cover a reader that holds no lock
      when the rewrite commits.  The nearest prior discussion found is
      "Logical replication is missing block of rows when sending initial
      sync?" (ZT/SC0A3+9tIE8qi@depesz.com), which has not been read.
      That, and a check of whether the same window exists for a
      subscription REFRESH rather than only initial CREATE, are what
      this needs before it goes to a list.

  update_missing from a tablesync worker, with every fix in place

      113_subscription fails roughly one run in seven at stressval 4
      with

        conflict detected on relation "public.pgbench_tellers":
        conflict=update_missing

      logged by a tablesync worker -- origin pg_NNNNN_NNNNN, not the
      main apply origin.  A tablesync worker only exists here because
      the subscription environment resynchronises pgbench_tellers by
      dropping it from the publication and adding it back, so the first
      suspicion should be that machinery rather than the server: the
      resync truncates the subscriber's copy so the fresh COPY has
      somewhere to land, and waits for catchup first so that changes
      decoded before the drop cannot arrive after the truncate.  Some
      window is evidently still open.

      The entry above is a candidate for which window.  The resync adds
      pgbench_tellers back to the publication, which starts a fresh
      tablesync for it, and REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) pgbench_tellers is in
      the DDL set -- so the same pre-COPY window is open on tellers for
      the same reason.  That would make this the harness merely creating
      the conditions rather than the harness being at fault.  Worth
      testing by checking whether the tellers failures also leave
      srsubstate='r' on an empty table.

      This matters for reading the rest of this file.  The CF 5151 entry
      was measured at two failures in eight with the fix reverted and
      none in eleven with it restored; the second half of that is now
      known to be luckier than it looked, since the scenario fails at
      about this rate for a reason unrelated to that fix.  The reverted
      rate stands -- it is a different and much higher rate, on a
      different relation, from the apply worker rather than a tablesync
      one -- but a future measurement here should separate the two by
      which origin logs the conflict.


Things this suite has produced that are not explained yet.  They are
here rather than in a commit message because an unreproduced failure is
a lead, not a result.

  REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) not getting its final lock under write load

      REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) pgbench_tellers waited for
      AccessExclusiveLock on that table for 185 seconds and then died on
      the lock timeout, with 23 backends queued behind it for
      RowExclusiveLock the whole time.  The workload was thirty clients
      of tpcb_like, whose transactions are a handful of statements and
      commit in milliseconds -- there was no long-running transaction
      for it to be waiting on.

      Reproduce with:

        PG_TEST_EXTRA='stress_mode=soak stress_seed=27072026
                       stress_soak_skip=19 stress_soak_count=1
                       stress_concurrently=2'

      which failed once in six here; at the default duration it took a
      whole slice to appear once.  The scenario is pgbench plus the
      generated decorator, standalone, repack_concurrently as the only
      command.  Nothing about it is exotic: the notable part is how
      ordinary it is.

      What is not established is whether this is starvation or simply a
      long wait that would have ended.  A request that has queued should
      be reached once the transactions ahead of it drain, and these
      drain in milliseconds, so 185 seconds wants explaining -- the
      shapes worth ruling out are a lock upgrade that does not queue
      fairly (REPACK holds ShareUpdateExclusiveLock on the table
      already and is asking to upgrade), and a wait that is retried
      rather than queued.  Both would be characteristics of the feature
      rather than bugs in it, but a command that can block every writer
      for three minutes and then give up is worth the REPACK thread
      knowing about either way.

      Related to the entry under "where the suite falls short" about
      commands starved by workloads that never leave a gap, but not the
      same: the others involved a workload that genuinely held locks
      across pauses, and this one does not.

  a backend that went away with nothing in its log

      pgbench: error: client 6 aborted in command 8 (SQL) of script 1;
      perhaps the backend died while processing

      Seen once, at stress_seed=555 stress_soak_skip=89, in a wal_replica
      scenario at scale 10 running reindex_table_concurrently and
      alter_table_rewrite.  The server log for that node has no ERROR,
      no FATAL, no TRAP and no signal: the only entry near the abort is
      the harness shutting the node down afterwards.  Three re-runs of
      the same index were clean.

      Treat with suspicion before treating as a finding.  The machine
      this ran on had taken the whole WSL environment down earlier the
      same day, and a backend killed from outside leaves exactly this
      trace -- pgbench sees the connection go, the server logs nothing
      because it was never asked to.  Worth watching for a second
      occurrence on a machine that has not just crashed.

  a COMMIT decoded before CLOG records it

      Not found here -- reported on the lists, and open at the time of
      writing as CF 6840:

        https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/flat/85833.1768840165@localhost

      RecordTransactionCommit() writes WAL before updating CLOG, so
      DecodeCommit() can call SnapBuildAddCommittedTxn() for a
      transaction that is still in progress as far as CLOG and the
      procarray are concerned.  A snapshot built from that has the xact
      as committed while TransactionIdDidCommit() does not, which gives
      wrong data on the subscriber and wrongly-set commit hint bits on
      the publisher.  The proposed fix waits in
      SnapBuildBuildSnapshot() until CLOG agrees.

      This is worth a line here because it is the shape this suite is
      built for and it is not obvious the suite would catch it: the
      window is at commit rather than around any one command, and the
      damage on the publisher is hint bits rather than an invariant.
      expect: any scenario running REPACK (CONCURRENTLY), which decodes
              its own table's changes and so builds these snapshots
              without a subscription being involved at all --
              repack_dml_s1 and overlapping_ddl first.  A subscription
              is a way to reach it, not the way: the thread reached it
              by running REPACK.
      status: tried, not reproduced.  20 scenario runs of repack_dml_s1
              and overlapping_ddl at stressval 4, plus 8 of the
              subscription scenario, all clean.  That is not evidence
              the bug is absent: the window is between XLogFlush()
              returning and TransactionIdCommitTree() completing, a few
              instructions wide, and nothing in these scenarios widens
              it.  Reaching it plausibly needs an injection point rather
              than load, which is worth knowing before spending more on
              it.

  could not open relation with OID, from the RI fast path

      Seen once, in CI on 2026-07-27 (GitHub Actions run 30289192531,
      Linux - Meson 64-bit, which builds with ASAN and cassert and runs
      with io_method=io_uring).  Every other job was green, and the
      stress suite passes locally on the same commit.

        ERROR:  could not open relation with OID 16435
        STATEMENT:  UPDATE pgb_child SET pid = 952
        BACKTRACE:  index_open <- ri_triggers <- RI_FKey_check_upd
                    <- AfterTriggerEndQuery <- standard_ExecutorFinish

      That is the RI fast path opening a conindid that no longer exists,
      which is what 02483bebd3c on this branch is supposed to prevent by
      re-reading the constraint under the PK table's lock.  So this is a
      hole in that fix, not a test problem.  There are only two
      index_open(riinfo->conindid) call sites and both carry the
      re-read.

      Timeline from the server log: REINDEX TABLE CONCURRENTLY
      pgb_parent ran at 13.299 and that backend was back to ordinary
      load statements by 14.202, so it had finished; the failing UPDATE
      started at 14.285.  The old index was already gone and no lock
      protected it, so this is not a race the lock interlock could have
      covered.

      One explanation was proposed and then falsified.  It was that the
      re-read is a no-op: ri_LoadConstraintInfo() returns the cached
      RI_ConstraintInfo without consulting pg_constraint whenever the
      entry is still marked valid, so a session holding a pre-swap
      conindid would keep it.  A deterministic probe was built for it
      with the injection points REINDEX CONCURRENTLY already has --
      warm a session's RI cache, pin the rebuild at
      reindex-relation-concurrently-before-swap or -before-set-dead,
      let it finish, then have the session do another RI check.  Six
      scenarios, including the same shapes with REPACK (CONCURRENTLY)
      and with the writing session holding its transaction open across
      the rebuild.  All clean: the invalidation is processed when the
      next lock is taken, and the re-read does see the new conindid.

      The batched path was then excluded too.  The CI statement was
      "UPDATE pgb_child SET pid = 952" with no WHERE, so every row fires
      a check and they go through ri_FastPathBatchAdd() with the flush
      at AfterTriggerEndQuery, which is where the CI stack ends; and the
      rebuild in flight was REINDEX TABLE CONCURRENTLY, not REINDEX
      INDEX.  Both were added to the probe -- whole-table updates on
      both sides of the rebuild, driven by REINDEX TABLE -- and all
      scenarios stayed clean.

      What is left, and what the reasoning keeps running into: for a
      backend to hold a dropped conindid it has to have read it before
      the swap, and having read it before the swap it holds RowShareLock
      on the referenced table from before the swap, so phase 5's
      WaitForLockers blocks the drop until it commits.  Every ordering
      that produces the failure seems to require the read to have
      happened without the lock -- which is what the fix addresses, and
      what the probe says does not happen.  Something in that chain is
      wrong and the local reproductions cannot say which link.

      So the next occurrence is instrumented to say it itself.  Both
      call sites now go through ri_OpenConstraintIndex(), which checks
      that the index still exists before opening it and, if it does not,
      reports the conindid it believed, the constraint, the referenced
      relation, and what pg_constraint says now.  The last of those is
      the interesting one: if it names a different index, the cached
      constraint info was stale and the re-read did not take; if it
      names the same missing index, the catalog itself points at a
      dropped index and the problem is in the rebuild, not in the RI
      code.  CI is the reproducer here -- it has produced this once and
      local runs have not produced it at all.

      Older notes on what the probe did not cover: the batched path.  ri_FastPathBatchAdd()
      queues up to RI_FASTPATH_BATCH_SIZE rows and the entry built by
      ri_FastPathGetEntry() is flushed at AfterTriggerEndQuery, which is
      where the CI stack ends up -- so the interval between building
      that entry and using it spans many rows, and is much longer than
      anything the probe exercised.  Note also that REPACK preserves
      index OIDs where REINDEX does not, so the OID that failed came
      from a REINDEX.

      Local reproduction: 8 runs of foreign_keys at stressval 4, clean,
      on a freshly installed build.  CI runs this under ASAN, which is
      far slower, and that is the likeliest reason it shows there.

  invalid arbiter index list -- diagnosed and fixed, 2026-07-28

      Seen three times over months, never reproducible on demand, and
      chased through several wrong theories before the answer came from
      instrumenting rather than guessing.

      What settled it was logging the arithmetic on every mapping rather
      than only on failure.  Over one run of partitions the check runs
      more than thirty thousand times, and the states it sees are:

        nidx=2 root=2 matched=2 additional=0    30537
        nidx=3 root=2 matched=2 additional=0      664
        nidx=4 root=2 matched=2 additional=2      646
        nidx=3 root=2 matched=2 additional=1      559

      So the fallback for indexes being concurrently rebuilt is
      exercised constantly and matched always equals the root's count.
      The only way to a mismatch is matched=0, which means no index on
      the partition has an arbiter among its ancestors -- and that is
      what a partition looks like once it has been detached: it keeps
      its indexes, and they keep enforcing their constraints, but
      pg_inherits no longer ties them to the parent's.  Routing a tuple
      to such a partition is legal, because DETACH ... CONCURRENTLY
      waits for the transactions that can still see it.

      In that state ExecInitPartitionInfo could not do anything sensible.
      Its fallback matches unparented indexes against arbiters already
      found *on the same partition*, and is guarded on there being some;
      with none, it did not run at all, and the count check then failed.

      The fix adds map_unparented_to_root_arbiters(), which matches the
      partition's parentless indexes against the root's arbiters
      directly, comparing definitions through an attribute map the way
      ATTACH PARTITION does.  Two details cost a round trip each:
      IsIndexCompatibleAsArbiter() cannot be used, because it asserts
      both indexes belong to the same relation; and an index matched
      this way must count towards the expected total rather than as an
      "additional" arbiter, since it stands in for the root arbiter
      rather than supplementing it.

      Verified by fault injection -- forcing get_partition_ancestors()
      to return NIL for one partition reproduces the error exactly:

        ERROR:  invalid arbiter index list for partition "p1": the root
        has 2 arbiter index(es), but 0 matched here (plus 2 being
        concurrently rebuilt), out of 2 index(es) on the partition

      With the fix and the same injection, the upsert takes its UPDATE
      path on an existing key, inserts a new one, and a plain INSERT of
      a duplicate still raises "duplicate key value violates unique
      constraint" -- so the arbiter is doing its job rather than being
      skipped.  regress, test_misc/010_index_concurrently_upsert,
      injection_points/isolation and amcheck/002_cic all pass, as does
      the stress suite, including partitions at stressval 4 and
      partitioned_pgbench at stressval 8.

      Seen once, in the partitions scenario during a soak run:

        ERROR:  invalid arbiter index list
        CONTEXT:  SQL statement "INSERT INTO pgb_part VALUES (p_id, 0)
                  ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET val = pgb_part.val"

      That elog is execPartition.c:883, reached when the number of
      arbiter indexes found for a partition does not match the root's
      plus the ones being concurrently rebuilt.  e6d6e32f424 fixed one
      way of getting there; this tree contains it.

      Seed 43565523, soak run of 2026-07-26, scenario partitions at
      stressval 1 with 20 clients.  Replaying that seed did not
      reproduce it, and neither did 30 runs of the same scenario, 12
      runs of a rotation narrowed to reindex plus the detach variants,
      or 10 runs at stressval 3.

      Seen twice more on 2026-07-27, in a control run at stressval 4 --
      eight runs with dbca3469ebf restored, which were supposed to be
      clean and were not.  That looked like a rate of 2 in 8 and it is
      not one: everything since has been clean.  Do not quote 2 in 8.

      What has been run since, all on a tree carrying e6d6e32f424,
      bc32a12e0db and 2bc7e886fc1, and all clean:

        - partitions, 10 runs at stressval 4 and 10 at stressval 8
        - partitions, 6 runs each with two different sleeps compiled in
          (see below), and 12 runs with the check downgraded to a
          WARNING so a run would not stop at the first sighting
        - a standalone driver outside the suite: 16 clients upserting
          through a three-partition parent while one loop detached and
          re-attached a partition (762 completed cycles), another
          rebuilt the parent's primary key with REINDEX CONCURRENTLY,
          and a third did the documented partitionwise index build
        - one soak pass, 14 scenario combinations, at stressval 4

      Two sleeps were compiled into the ancestor loop in
      ExecInitPartitionInfo to widen the window, on the theory that the
      leaf's catalog reads see a state the planner's arbiter list does
      not.  The first went next to the injection point inside the loop
      and was useless: on a leaf with one index it fires after the only
      ancestor lookup.  The second went before the loop, which does
      widen the interval between the root's arbiter list and the leaf's
      reads.  Neither produced a sighting, so that theory is neither
      confirmed nor excluded -- only that 2ms of it is not enough.

      The mid-loop hypothesis is now excluded, by experiment rather
      than by argument.  The idea was that the leaf's catalog reads see
      a state the planner's arbiter list does not, because
      get_partition_ancestors() refreshes the catalog snapshot each time
      round the loop (the XXX comment above it says so).  The loop has
      an injection point in it,
      exec-init-partition-after-get-partition-ancestors, so a backend
      can be held there while another session tries the DDL.  Four
      commands were tried, each with lock_timeout so a block shows up as
      an error rather than a hang:

        REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY <partitioned pkey>
                                        ShareLock on the relation
        REINDEX INDEX CONCURRENTLY <leaf pkey>
                                        ShareLock on a virtual xact
        ALTER TABLE .. DETACH PARTITION .. CONCURRENTLY
                                        ShareLock on a virtual xact
        ALTER INDEX .. ATTACH PARTITION <child index>
                                        AccessExclusiveLock on the index

      All four blocked on locks the executing backend already holds, and
      the upsert came out clean every time.  So no catalog change that
      could disturb the mapping can commit while a backend is inside the
      loop; the window this file spent 44 runs and two compiled-in
      sleeps trying to widen does not exist.

      That relocates the race to before the statement takes its locks:
      the arbiter list is decided when the plan is made, and the leaf's
      ancestors are read when the executor first opens that partition.
      A skew between the two has to be established across a statement
      boundary, not inside one -- which fits the scenario that produces
      it, since partitions is the one running force_generic_plan with
      the prepared protocol against an upsert wrapped in a plpgsql
      function, so its plans are cached and revalidated rather than
      built fresh.  The shape to try next: cache the plan in one
      session, let REINDEX CONCURRENTLY on the *partitioned* index
      complete from another while the first sits idle, then execute the
      cached plan.  That rebuild gives the parent index a new OID, so a
      plan still naming the old one would find no leaf index descended
      from it -- matched = 0 against a root list of 1, which is exactly
      the failing arithmetic.

      Something else that was never exercised until 2026-07-27: an
      arbiter list with more than one index in it.  Every ON CONFLICT
      here resolved to a primary key, and the only UNIQUE index in the
      suite was the matview's, so the root list this check counts
      against was always a single entry -- meaning the code that maps
      several arbiters onto a partition, dedupes them by parent and
      counts the ones being concurrently rebuilt had nothing to do.
      upsert_keys and partitioned_side now each carry a second unique
      index over the same column as the primary key, so EXPLAIN reports

        Conflict Arbiter Indexes: p_pkey, pgb_part_id_uniq

      on both the plain and the partitioned target.  8 runs of
      partitions at stressval 4 with that in place were clean, so it did
      not immediately reach the residual, but it is the arithmetic the
      failing check performs and it is now being driven.

      Reached again on 2026-07-27, and this time by a change to the
      suite rather than by luck: one run in eight of partitions at
      stressval 4, in a control run made after adding the
      partition_upsert_contend load.  That load races several clients to
      insert the same absent key through the parent, which is the first
      time anything here drove speculative insertion on a partitioned
      table.  A following batch of ten was clean, so call it rare and
      bursty rather than one-in-eight; but a load that reaches it at all
      is more than this file had before, and it is the thing to run when
      picking this up again.

      The improved message did its job and was then thrown away: the
      failure named pgb_part_4, and the counts that follow it were lost
      because the next run in the loop overwrote the server log.  When
      looping for this, copy
      testrun/stress/106_partitions/log/*_partitions.log aside on every
      failure -- meson keeps only the last run's.

      Recreating the instrumentation takes two minutes: in
      execPartition.c, above the count check, build a StringInfo naming
      partrel, then every entry of leaf_part_rri->ri_IndexRelationDescs
      with its indisvalid/indisready/indislive and the OIDs
      get_partition_ancestors() returns for it, then the root's arbiter
      OIDs, and elog it.  Downgrade the elog to WARNING so one run can
      record more than one sighting.

      Since revert-testing e6d6e32f424 (3 failures in 8 without it,
      against this one sighting in 400-odd soak combinations with it),
      the shape is confirmed as the one that commit addresses -- so this
      is either a residual case it does not cover, or something that
      reaches the same elog by another route.

      One hypothesis worth testing before assuming a server bug: the
      detach_drop_recreate_partition variant attaches a partition whose
      index was created by CREATE TABLE ... INCLUDING ALL, so there is a
      window where a child index exists without a parent for a reason
      other than REINDEX CONCURRENTLY -- which is the shape the arbiter
      matching reasons about.  If that is what it is, it is still
      reachable by an ordinary ATTACH PARTITION and still an internal
      error, so it is worth reporting either way.


What a survey run found
=======================

Soak at stress_seconds=1 -- a combination costs what its cluster costs
to build and nothing more -- over four slices of forty combinations,
seed 4242, on 2026-07-28.  It is the run to do after adding plugins,
and it earned that reputation: three combinations could not run at all,
and none of the three would ever have appeared in the catalogue, which
pins its own plugin lists.

  Checks that name a table without declaring the schema that
  creates it.  repack_refuses_deferrable and deferred_keys_intact use
  pgb_defer, and pgstat_rejects_invalid_index uses pgb_bare; invented
  combinations paired all three with schemas that have neither, and the
  run died on "relation pgb_defer does not exist".  Every check written
  before this session declared its schema; the ones added during it did
  not.  Fixed by declaring it.

  Loads that break an invariant they do not declare a conflict with.
  balances sums accounts, tellers, branches and history and requires
  all four to agree.  history_insert writes deltas into history with no
  balancing rows, and hot_churn moves abalance alone; both are correct
  in the scenarios that use them, because those scenarios do not run
  balances -- and soak does not know that.  Fixed by declaring the
  conflict.

  A check that assumed dropping one index cannot remove another.
  invalid_indexes_droppable lists the invalid indexes and then drops
  them one at a time; a transient index belonging to a partitioned one
  goes away with its parent, and the next drop failed with "index
  ... does not exist".  Fixed with IF EXISTS.

A second pass over the same four slices, after those fixes, found four
more and then came back clean.

  A conflict declared under the wrong name, which is worse than no
  conflict at all.  The registry key for checks is "checks"; the two
  conflicts added above said "check", so the lookup went to a spec key
  that does not exist and the conflict never fired -- soak went on
  building the combination the plugin had just declared it could not be
  part of, and the balances failure came back unchanged.  Corrected,
  and _validate() now rejects a requires or conflicts naming a kind
  that is not in the registry, so the next typo of this shape fails
  loudly instead of silently permitting everything.

  Environments that cannot answer anything in a second.  subscription
  and standby have to get a second server caught up before their checks
  mean anything; at stress_seconds=1 they reported a subscriber that
  was simply still behind.  Both now declare min_seconds and survey
  mode respects it.

  Two benign server warnings the stderr matchers did not allow.
  REINDEX CONCURRENTLY says so and skips when it meets an exclusion
  constraint index, in the run's own matcher and again in the
  cancellation environment's.  Both now tolerate it, as they already
  did for the invalid-index skip.

  temp_table_cic against the cancellation environment.  That
  environment picks its victims by matching query text against
  REPACK|REINDEX|CREATE INDEX|DROP INDEX, and this load runs CREATE
  INDEX CONCURRENTLY from a workload client -- so it terminated a
  writer, and the run failed on a writer that died, which is not what
  that environment is testing.  Declared as a conflict.

A third pass, on the final tree, ran all four slices again: 0, 40 and 80
are clean, and 120 fails on one combination, invented_126, which it
does every time.

That one is contention rather than incompatibility, and it is worth
describing because the obvious fix made things worse.  The combination
is scale 1 with twenty clients, tpcb_like among its loads, and a
rotation of add_validate_constraint and reindex_schema_concurrently.
At scale 1 there is one pgbench_branches row and ten teller rows and
every tpcb transaction updates both, so a client that blocks behind DDL
while holding one of those row locks takes the whole client set with
it: the log shows ten waiters on RowExclusiveLock and the victim
waiting on "ShareLock on transaction", which is a row lock, until the
30-second lock timeout fires.

Bounding the constraint statements helped and was kept:
add_validate_constraint's ADD and DROP both need AccessExclusiveLock,
and they now go through pgb_ddl_bounded() -- the helper written for
ATTACH PARTITION, renamed since it is not attach-specific -- so a
failed attempt releases the queue instead of parking at the head of it.
It did not fix this combination.

Raising the invented scale when the client count is high was tried and
reverted.  It removes the hot row, but two-thirds of combinations then
build and check five times the data: a slice that took four minutes was
still on its fifteenth combination after twenty, and one of them hung
past the budget.  Soak buys breadth, and that trade sells it.  The
change also shifts which combination each index names, which costs the
one property that makes a soak failure reportable.

So invented_126 stands as a known failure of survey mode rather than of
the server or the plugins.  Anyone reproducing it should know it is a
lock timeout behind a hot row, not a combination that cannot run.


A bug fixed on this branch only
===============================

Everything above is a fix that already exists upstream, put back to see
whether this suite notices.  This one is in between: the bug is still
open in master, the proposed fix is applied on this branch, and the
scenario below guards it exactly as the entries above guard theirs --
revert the fix and the suite fails.

  "Race conditions in logical decoding", Antonin Houska
  https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/flat/85833.1768840165@localhost
  commitfest 6840, needs review

      RecordTransactionCommit() writes the commit record, flushes it,
      and only then sets the transaction's status in CLOG.  The flush is
      what lets a decoder see the commit, so between the two there is a
      window where a transaction is committed as far as logical decoding
      is concerned and not committed as far as CLOG is concerned.  A
      snapshot built from decoded commits leaves such a transaction out
      of its running list, so an ordinary visibility check reads it as
      neither running nor committed -- which HeapTupleSatisfiesMVCC
      treats as aborted, and writes into the hint bits, so the wrong
      answer outlives the window.

      REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) is the vector this suite already had:
      repack_worker.c builds its snapshot with SnapBuildInitialSnapshot()
      and the backend copies the old heap under it, so a row version
      whose writer is caught in the window is dropped from the copy and
      marked dead in the original.  Antonin found the bug the same way --
      stress-testing REPACK.

      expect: decoding_startup_race (balances).  The scenario is a tight
              REPACK loop over the two smallest hot pgbench tables, with
              far more clients than cores.
      revert: the fix commit on this branch, "Fix race conditions during
              the setup of logical decoding".  Nothing has to be edited
              by hand: the scenario declares the 'decoding' chaos
              profile, which attaches jitter to the
              commit-before-clog-update injection point and widens the
              window for one commit in eight.
      status: CATCHES, 4 failures in 5 runs at stress_seconds=20 and 3 in
              3 at the default duration, as

                not ok - balances agree across all four tables
                balances disagree: accounts=53172 tellers=53172
                                   branches=56806 history=53172

              A row version dropped by the copy takes one table out of
              step with the other three for good, which is the ordinary
              invariant noticing ordinary corruption -- no check had to
              be written for this.  With the fix applied, 3 runs
              in 3 are clean under the same profile, which is what says
              this is that bug and not another one.

      how wide the window has to be: 10ms fails, 5ms does not.  That is
              not arbitrary.  The stall has to outlast the gap between
              the decoding worker building the snapshot and the backend
              reaching the first tuple with it, and that gap is 6-15ms:
              the snapshot goes out through a file and the backend has to
              wake up and read it.

      why it is not caught unaided: the natural window is far too small.
              Measured over the same scenario at 300 clients on 16 cores,
              instrumenting the CLOG update with instr_time: in 60
              seconds only 7 commits took 50us or more between the flush
              and the CLOG update, 3 took more than 500us, and the widest
              seen was 10.3ms.  So nature does produce a window of the
              needed size, roughly once a minute, and it then has to land
              in the 15ms before one of the ~30 snapshots a minute --
              about one run in two hours at this exposure rate, and no
              hit in the 134 exposures tried.  Raising it means more
              REPACKs in flight, which is why the scenario declares
              ddl_concurrency 2 and the two smallest tables.

              One idea that did not work, recorded so that it is not
              tried again: forcing running-xacts records from the
              workload with pg_log_standby_snapshot(), on the theory that
              they gate how often a decoder can reach a consistent point.
              Measured A/B at 300 clients with chaos off, 16 decoding
              startups in 30 seconds without it and 14 with.  They are
              not the limiter -- ReplicationSlotReserveWal() already logs
              one per slot created, and what a startup then waits for is
              the transactions running at that moment to end.

              The other consumer of SnapBuildInitialSnapshot() is the
              tablesync of logical replication, where the snapshot is
              used in the same session one round trip later rather than
              through a file.  That gap is a few hundred microseconds, so
              it needs a stall two orders of magnitude smaller -- the
              kind that does happen several times a minute.  That is
              where an unaided reproduction should be attempted next.

Another bug fixed on this branch only, and a hole that was in the fix
=====================================================================

  "[SP-]GiST IOS visibility bug", Matthias van de Meent
  https://www.postgresql.org/message-id/flat/CAEze2WgH13m=MDST58KLo-NkZpbwBEt4xNWcgtghWBwRj3J0+A@mail.gmail.com
  commitfest 5721, needs review

  Still open in master.  The v02 series from the thread is carried on
  this branch, together with the correction below, so the scenario runs
  with the rest of the catalogue and what it guards is the revert.

      GiST and SP-GiST index scans do not interlock with their own index
      vacuum the way btree does.  A scan reads a page, queues the TIDs on
      it and lets the page go; vacuum is then free to remove those index
      entries, remove the heap tuples they pointed at, and mark the pages
      all-visible.  When the scan returns a queued TID, the index-only
      path sees an all-visible page and returns the value from the index
      without looking at the heap -- so a row deleted before the scan's
      snapshot began comes back.

      expect: ios_vacuum_race, whose invariant is inside the workload:
              the scan and a read of the same table run under one
              repeatable-read snapshot, so they can only disagree if the
              scan returned something that is not there.
      revert: either the v02 commits on this branch -- "Expose visibility
              checking shim for index usage" and the GiST and SP-GiST
              ones -- or, for the SP-GiST half alone, "SP-GiST: check the
              visibility of the tuple that is actually returned".
      status: CATCHES, 5 failures in 5 runs at stress_seconds=20 against
              unpatched master, on both access methods, as

                index-only scan on pgb_ios_gist returned 7 rows the heap
                does not have, out of 207: {"(139,1)","(62,3)"}

              and 4 runs in 4 clean with the fix and the correction, both
              at stress_seconds=20 and at the default duration.

      what it found in the fix: with the v02 series applied as posted,
              GiST went clean and SP-GiST still leaked -- 6 runs in 6,
              sorted and unsorted alike.  spgLeafTest() checked the
              visibility of item->heapPtr and attributed the answer to
              the tuple it then returns, which is leafTuple->heapPtr.
              Those are the same only when the search item is itself a
              leaf; for a descent from an inner tuple, item->heapPtr
              holds an index block pointer, so the visibility map was
              consulted for an index block as though it were a heap
              block.  Checking leafTuple->heapPtr instead makes 4 runs in
              4 clean.  That correction is a commit of its own on this
              branch and has been sent to the thread.

              The author's own isolation tests pass against the
              uncorrected patch, and the reason looks like table size:
              ten narrow rows fit a single SP-GiST page, so the scan
              never descends from an inner tuple and item->heapPtr is
              never the wrong kind of pointer.  This scenario uses two
              hundred rows at fillfactor 10.

      controls: the same scan with enable_indexonlyscan off is clean in
              3 runs of 3, which is what says the rows come from the
              index-only path and not from the check being wrong.  An
              earlier version of the check compared counts rather than
              values and was itself wrong: the churn re-inserted the same
              point it deleted, so a revived row and its replacement were
              indistinguishable to a count.  Every row version now takes
              a coordinate from a sequence.

A bug this suite found
======================

  Do not hold rd_smgr across the fork loop when enabling data checksums

      ProcessSingleRelationByOid() fetched the smgr handle once and then
      read rel->rd_smgr on each iteration of the loop over forks.  A
      relcache invalidation resets that field to NULL, and the body of
      the loop invites one -- it reads catalogs, pins buffers and checks
      for interrupts -- so the next iteration passed NULL to smgrexists()
      and the worker segfaulted.

      found by: 127_data_checksums_crash, at 2 failures in 10 runs.
      status: FIXED on this branch, 0 in 10 after.

      How it was narrowed is the part worth keeping.  The first sighting
      was under a chaos profile that both jittered five injection points
      and turned on the probabilistic cache discard, which is two
      explanations at once.  Turning chaos off entirely gave 0 in 12, so
      something in the profile was needed; running the cache discard on
      its own gave 2 in 10, which named it.  The jitter was never
      implicated, and the two injection points added to the worker's page
      loop for this hunt cannot fire on the relations that crashed --
      they have no blocks, so the loop those points sit in never runs.

      That mattered, because instrumentation added for a hunt is the
      first thing to suspect when the hunt finds something.  Ruling it
      out is what turned this into a report rather than a curiosity: the
      final reproduction has no stress suite, no concurrency and no
      injection points in it at all, just

        initdb --no-data-checksums
        postgres -c debug_discard_caches=1
        SELECT pg_enable_data_checksums(0, 10000);

      which is the stock debugging GUC an assert build is expected to
      survive.

  hash: do not hold the cached metapage across hashbulkdelete's scan

      hashbulkdelete() held the relcache's own copy of the metapage --
      rel->rd_amcache, as returned by _hash_getcachedmetap() -- across
      its loop over buckets, and handed the same pointer to the read
      stream callback.  The loop locks buffers and accepts invalidation
      messages, and an invalidation for the index pfrees rd_amcache, so
      BUCKET_TO_BLKNO() went on computing block numbers out of freed
      memory:

        ERROR:  could not open file "base/5/16441.1" (target block
                960051515): previous segment is only 760 blocks
        CONTEXT:  while scanning relation "public.pgbench_accounts"
        STATEMENT:  VACUUM pgbench_accounts;

      found by: 116_access_methods, at 4 failures in 12 runs under the
              discard_hard profile.
      status: FIXED on this branch, 0 in 12 after.

      Two details made this findable.  The block number differs on every
      occurrence, which is what distinguishes freed memory from a value
      that is merely stale; and backtrace_functions on _mdfd_getseg named
      the caller in one run, which turned "something asked for a silly
      block" into hashbulkdelete in a parallel vacuum worker.  Neither
      was guesswork, and neither cost more than a few minutes.

      It is the same class as the data checksum crash above: a pointer
      into a cache held across something that can invalidate the cache.
      That is worth saying plainly, because it suggests where to look
      next rather than only what was found.

Where the suite falls short
===========================

Most of what the first draft of this list found has been closed; what
follows is what is left.

 1. Nothing takes predicate locks, so the SSI entries above are
    uncovered.  This is a regression against the old suite -- see
    PORTING -- and the replacement wants an isolation test rather than
    another scenario.

 2. Nothing compares a dump against its source, so the pg_dump entry is
    uncovered.  This one fits the check registry and should come back
    as a plugin.

 3. No deferrable primary keys.  REPACK refuses those rather than
    mishandling them now, so covering 832e220d99a means asserting a
    clean refusal, which is a regression test rather than a stress
    shape.

 4. Nothing builds an index whose expression reads a second table, so
    5bbdfa8a18d stays out of reach.  Upstream needed injection points
    for it, and probably so would this.

 5. Three dimensions cannot be combined with the partitioned decorator:
    an exclusion constraint, because one on a partitioned table has to
    contain the partition key; a foreign key, because the recipe that
    partitions an existing table would leave it pointing at the
    partition rather than the parent; and the subscription environment,
    which builds an index of its own on the subscriber and drops it
    concurrently, which a partitioned index refuses.  All three are
    declared conflicts, so soak skips those combinations rather than
    failing them -- but partitioning plus referential integrity, and
    partitioning plus logical replication, are real shapes that are not
    covered.

 6. Two things make a partitioned scenario die on a lock timeout three
    minutes in, having tested nothing, and both are now fixed rather
    than tolerated.

    The first is an unbounded ATTACH PARTITION.  It needs
    AccessExclusiveLock on the partition, the load writes to that
    partition continuously, and so the request parks at the head of the
    lock queue while every writer stacks up behind it -- until the lock
    timeout fires for all of them at once.  Every ATTACH in the suite
    now goes through pgb_attach_bounded(), which retries the command in
    500ms slices: a failed attempt releases the queue and lets it drain,
    so the worst case is half a second of stall instead of the whole
    timeout.  The detaches that pair with those attaches are guarded by
    a check that the partition is still attached, so a turn that could
    not get it back on heals on the next one rather than failing on "is
    not a partition".  Measured on partitioned_pgbench: 1 failure in 6
    before, 0 in 12 after, at the same twenty clients, with the slowest
    passing run going from 197s to 11s.

    The second is row-lock contention, and it only shows up when the
    whole suite runs at once.  At scale 1 there is a single
    pgbench_branches row and every transaction updates it, so the
    clients queue on that row; the longer the queue, the more certain it
    is that a DDL command parking a lock request turns into a
    three-minute cascade.  partitioned_pgbench runs ten clients rather
    than twenty for that reason.  Three consecutive full-suite runs are
    clean with both changes.

 7. Several entries above are only observable under assertions.  The
    suite should be run against an assertion build as a matter of
    course; nothing enforces that today.

 8. The catalogue runs at six seconds a scenario by default.  Every
    entry above that says "untested" needs a revert run to become
    "catches", and the two findings this suite has made on its own both
    came out of soak mode rather than the default run.  Soak needs to
    run somewhere on a schedule for any of this to hold.

 9. Indexing the column the load moves is what makes a scenario
    interesting, and it is also what switched off an entire mechanism.
    An update is HOT only when no index covers a column it changes, so
    for as long as every scenario declared an index on abalance, no
    update anywhere in the suite was a HOT update and no HOT chain was
    ever pruned -- which is the whole of what d9d0762 broke.  The blind
    spot was not one scenario forgetting something; it was the same
    reasonable choice made everywhere.  Worth asking of any mechanism
    the server has: does something in the standard shape of these
    scenarios turn it off?

10. Until the triggers scenario, nothing here ran a statement from a
    plan it had not just parsed.  Every load is pgbench SQL, re-parsed
    on each execution, so a plan could only ever be built against the
    catalog as it stood; a session that holds a plan across a rebuild --
    and has to be made to replan when the index that plan chose is
    swapped or dropped underneath it -- was not represented at all.
    That is the shape behind get_relation_info() opening an index replay
    had already removed, and it is what a plpgsql body has by
    construction: planned once, reused for the life of the session.
    trigger_audit puts three of them in the rotation's path -- the audit
    insert, the arbiter inference in the counter upsert, and the lookup
    audit_probe calls -- and alter_trigger_function invalidates all of
    them at once without taking a lock on any table.

    The same schema is also the only place where rows arrive from inside
    a trigger rather than from a statement pgbench sent: pgb_audit's
    indexes are maintained from the after-trigger queue, and a deferred
    constraint trigger reads each row back at commit time, through an
    index a rebuild may have swapped since the insert.

    None of it has been shown to catch anything yet.  It closes a shape
    the suite was missing, which is not the same as a revert that
    failed, and it carries the same "untested" status as the entries
    above until one does.

Duplicated upstream tests
=========================

Several tests in the tree cover the same ground as a scenario here, and
cost buildfarm time doing it.  Once a scenario is shown to catch the bug
an upstream test guards -- shown, by reverting the fix and watching this
suite fail -- that test is a candidate for removal, and the case for
removing it should be made on the same thread as this suite.

Two rules for that, both learned from the entries above:

  Only stress-shaped tests are candidates.  contrib/amcheck/t/002_cic.pl
  and 003_cic_2pc.pl are pgbench workloads racing a concurrent build,
  which is exactly what a scenario is, and one scenario can replace
  both.  The injection-point specs under src/test/modules are not:
  they are deterministic, they run in a second, and they fail the same
  way every time.  A probabilistic test never replaces a deterministic
  one, however much ground it covers.

  Only after the revert run.  Removing a test because a scenario "should"
  catch the bug is how coverage disappears quietly.  The entries in this
  file are the record of which ones have earned it.

  candidate                                     covered by
  --------------------------------------------- --------------------
  contrib/amcheck/t/002_cic.pl                  NOT a candidate; see
                                                fdd965d074d above.  It
                                                catches 2 in 2 what
                                                index_build_churn
                                                catches 2 in 8.
  contrib/amcheck/t/003_cic_2pc.pl              bulk_and_rewrite

Nothing has earned it yet: every entry above is still "untested".

Proposed on the REPACK thread, and worth matching rather than removing:
contrib/amcheck/t/007_repack_concurrently.pl and 008.  Both are pgbench
with an advisory-locked DDL client, which is this suite's own shape, so
the comparison is close:

  007: a two-column table with a secondary index, balanced-pair updates
       keeping SUM(j) constant, and the three REPACK forms.  Covered:
       +ledger with balanced_pair holds the same invariant,
       repack_concurrently and repack_using_index are the same commands.
       007 randomises whether the secondary index covers the updated
       column, which decides whether the updates can be HOT; here that
       is fixed per scenario rather than per run -- repack_dml_s1
       without btree_abalance is the HOT shape, with it the non-HOT one.

  008: inserts under an advisory lock taking values from a sequence, and
       COUNT(*) WHERE j <= last must equal last.  Covered: +gapless with
       serial_insert and gapless_count, the same invariant on
       pgbench_history.

  What neither was covered by until now: both run
  bt_index_parent_check(heapallindexed) inside the pgbench script,
  immediately after each REPACK, rather than once at the end.  That is
  the difference between "an index was corrupted somewhere in this run"
  and "this command corrupted it", and a later rewrite can repair the
  damage before a final check ever sees it.  repack_dml_s1 now sets
  verify_after_repack, which appends those checks to the repack
  variants.  One delta remains: 007 and 008 also verify the table's
  primary key, which this suite does not declare as an index and so
  cannot name.

Not a candidate: src/test/modules/test_misc/t/
010_index_concurrently_upsert.pl stays.  The unique_and_gapless and
partitions scenarios overlap it, but it is aimed at one thing, it names
what it is testing, and it is where the arbiter fixes above were
developed.  Overlap is not duplication when the narrower test is the one
that will say what broke.


Not a bug: "lost updates under parallel repeats" (2026-08-01)
============================================================

Recorded because the reasoning took an evening and the trap is easy to
fall into again.

While re-verifying the framework restructure, four concurrent copies of
122_decoding_startup_race appeared to lose updates on the small tables
repack_hot_small rewrites: the four-way balance broke 3 runs in 4, the
final check failed too -- so the damage outlived the workload, which
is the signature of the class this scenario exists for -- and it
reproduced with the jitter switched off and on the pre-restructure
framework alike.  It looked like a second window of the logical
decoding CLOG race.

It was not.  The four copies were being run as

  meson test stress/122_decoding_startup_race --repeat 4

and src/tools/testwrap begins every run with

  if os.path.exists(testdir) and os.path.isdir(testdir):
      shutil.rmtree(testdir)

where testdir is derived from the test's name and nothing else.  Four
concurrent repeats of one test therefore share one directory, and each
one deletes the other three's data directories -- PGDATA and all --
while their postmasters are running.  A cluster whose files are pulled
out from under it produces exactly the symptom above.

The discriminator: run the same four copies as four differently named
tests, so each gets its own directory, under the same machine load.
Sixteen such runs in four rounds all passed, against 3-in-4 failures
under --repeat.  Nothing about the server was involved.

The lesson, and the reason this is written down: --repeat is safe for a
test that only reads, and unsafe for any test that builds a cluster.
For concurrent copies of a cluster test, give each copy its own name.
Stress::Run now claims its test directory with a sentinel file and
fails with this explanation if it finds the claim gone or taken, so the
next person meets a message rather than a mystery.


Two things the first real soak over the derived chaos pool found
================================================================
(2026-08-02)

Both are the suite's own, which is what a hunt over invented
combinations is for: nothing here had been reachable before, because
each needs a combination no hand-written scenario names.

1.  A guard that could not be prepared, and a premise that was wrong.

    subscriber_delete_reinsert asserts, in the strict-MVCC form added
    when the tolerance became a declared property, that its delete
    under the advisory lock found exactly one row.  Two faults, one on
    top of the other:

      ERROR:  could not determine data type of parameter $2
      ERROR:  prepared statement "P_3" does not exist

    The first is the guard itself: format() takes VARIADIC "any", so a
    pgbench variable handed to it under the extended or prepared
    protocol arrives as an untyped parameter.  Every other check in the
    suite casts for this reason and carries a comment saying so; this
    one did not.  The second is the consequence -- pgbench believes a
    PREPARE that failed and goes on using the statement -- and is what
    ends the run.

    With the cast in place the guard fired honestly, and was still
    wrong: "delete under lock found 0 rows".  The premise was that a
    scenario whose rotation carries no repack has no MVCC gap, but the
    subscription topology repacks the subscriber's own copy of
    pgbench_accounts in its run loop, whatever the rotation does.  The
    gap is now derived from the axes as well as from the rotation --
    an axis declares mvcc_safe for itself -- and the subscription
    topology declares it.

    Reachable only as subscription + prepared protocol + a rotation
    with no repack in it, which is why the catalogue never saw it: 113
    repacks, so it always took the tolerant path.

2.  Two injection points that cannot be jittered at all.

    aio-process-completion-before-shared (aio.c) and
    aio-worker-after-reopen (method_worker.c) are plain INJECTION_POINT
    calls reached inside a critical section.  The first arrival in a
    backend does the work the macro pair exists to avoid -- creates the
    local cache, dlopens the callback -- and allocating there is fatal:

      TRAP: failed Assert("CritSectionCount == 0 ||
                           (context)->allowInCritSection"),
      File: "../src/backend/utils/mmgr/mcxt.c", Line: 1240
      LOG:  client backend was terminated by signal 6: Aborted

    Not a server bug: upstream's test_aio attaches to both and then
    calls InjectionPointLoad() in every backend, with the comment
    "Pre-load the injection points now, so we can call them in a
    critical section".  Jitter attached from SQL has no way to arrange
    that, so both points are excluded from the pool rather than capped
    low -- the first entries %CHAOS_EXCLUDED has needed, and exactly
    the case it was built for.

    Worth knowing for anyone else attaching to points generically: a
    site inside a critical section is usable only by a callback every
    backend pre-loads, and nothing in the catalogue marks which sites
    those are.

3.  A check that waited for something correct not to happen.

    decoding_disabled polls until effective_wal_level falls back to
    replica, which is what a REPACK's transient slot going away looks
    like.  Against a subscription it never does, and should not: the
    subscription owns a logical slot for as long as it exists, so
    decoding stays on because it is still wanted.  The check waited out
    its 192-second poll and reported the server doing its job:

      not ok - logical decoding was switched back off
      poll_query_until timed out: SELECT current_setting(
        'effective_wal_level') = 'replica'

    Unreachable before the environment axis was split, since
    wal_replica and subscription were two values of one axis and could
    not both be chosen; the first soak that could name both found it in
    ten combinations.  The check now conflicts with the subscription
    topology.  Its neighbour, the cancellation disruptor's final, polls
    the same thing and was already declared incompatible for the same
    reason.

4.  A rewrite the triggers forbid.

    alter_trigger_function pulls in the trigger_audit schema, whose
    audit trigger on pgbench_accounts carries

      WHEN (OLD.abalance IS DISTINCT FROM NEW.abalance)

    so abalance is a column used in a trigger definition, and
    alter_table_rewrite -- which changes exactly that column's type --
    is refused:

      ERROR:  cannot alter type of a column used in a trigger definition

    The server is right and says so plainly; the combination is simply
    not one to invent.  The generated schema already declared this
    conflict for the same class of reason (a stored generated column
    pins the type of what it reads), and trigger_audit now does too.

    Newly reachable because a command pulls its own schema in: before
    that, naming alter_trigger_function meant naming trigger_audit, and
    soak only offered commands whose schema was already chosen.

5.  A resync that could overrun the one before it.

    The subscription topology takes pgbench_tellers out of the
    publication, truncates it on the subscriber and puts it back, so
    that a table synchronization runs from scratch against a live
    workload.  It returned as soon as the refresh was issued, without
    waiting for the copy that refresh asks for -- so with a workload
    light enough for the cycles to come round quickly, the next cycle's
    TRUNCATE landed on a synchronization still in flight.  The copy put
    rows in, the truncate took them out, and the updates that followed
    arrived for rows the subscriber no longer had:

      not ok - no update_missing conflict was logged

    while all three table comparisons passed, because the cycle after
    that repaired the data.  Only the log remembered, which is why the
    check reads it.

    The cycle now waits for the subscription to finish syncing before
    going round again.  Worth noting what this says about the check:
    comparing the tables at the end would never have caught it, and the
    log line was the whole evidence.

Which lever to point at the whole catalogue (2026-08-02)
=======================================================

Recorded because it has now cost two people time, and the answer is not
the obvious one.

Forcing discard_hard -- the cache discard at ten times the ordinary
rate -- on every scenario at once starves the catalogue rather than
searching it.  One full-catalogue pass in eight ended with

  ERROR:  canceling statement due to lock timeout

in 109_plancache_and_matview, after REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) on
pgbench_tellers sat at the head of the lock queue for nine minutes
waiting for the AccessExclusiveLock its final swap needs, with every
writer stacked behind it.  Nothing about the server: forcing a catalog
flush at every opportunity costs about two orders of magnitude, and a
command that must briefly exclude every writer cannot get through a
queue that never drains.  The commit that introduced stress_chaos=
measured the same thing at the same rate -- one failure in twelve.

The first version of this note said discard_only was the answer, on
six clean passes.  It is not: the seventh starved
117_partitioned_pgbench the same way, and 117 is the scenario whose own
comment explains that at scale 5 every transaction queues on one
pgbench_branches row, so any parked lock request builds a queue.  One
pass in seven against one in eight is no difference at all.

What is true is narrower.  Forcing any profile that slows the server
materially will occasionally starve the scenarios tuned for one that
is not, whichever profile it is, and the useful skill is reading the
failure rather than avoiding it: a run that took ten times its duration
and ended in "canceling statement due to lock timeout" is starvation,
not a finding.  discard_only is still the rate the two backend bugs on
this branch were found at, so it remains the lever to point at the
catalogue -- with the expectation of a starved pass now and then, and
without treating one as a result.

A second symptom belongs to the same cause, and cost time twice before
being written down: under --protocol=prepared, a PREPARE cancelled by
lock_timeout leaves pgbench believing the statement was prepared, and
the next use of it fails with

  ERROR:  prepared statement "P_0" does not exist

That message is never the finding.  It always means an earlier error,
and the earlier error is the one to read -- a lock timeout here, an
untyped parameter in the case recorded above.

A subscriber left empty by a REPACK of the table being copied
=============================================================
(2026-08-02, worth taking to the REPACK thread)

The known gap -- "REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) is not MVCC-safe yet: a
snapshot spanning its relfilenode swap can find the table empty" -- has
a consequence outside this suite's own checks, and this is the first
time it has been seen there.  Logical replication's initial table
synchronization copies under a snapshot exported from a replication
slot, which is the second of the two places in the server where a
snapshot derived from decoded commits is used for ordinary MVCC
visibility.  If a REPACK of that table commits while the copy is
running, the copy can succeed having read nothing.

From one forced-chaos pass, all four lines from the same run:

  01:30:34.289  tablesync worker for "pgbench_branches" has started
  01:30:34.507  publisher: REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) pgbench_branches;
  01:30:35.772  tablesync worker for "pgbench_branches" has finished
  01:30:36.171  conflict detected on "public.pgbench_branches":
                confl_update_missing

and 491 such conflicts by the end of an eighteen-second run.  The
subscriber's copy of the table stayed empty: the sync is done, so
nothing re-copies it, and every change that follows is applied against
a table with no rows.  The final comparison caught it -- got '0|0',
expected '1|-71496' -- but a real subscriber has no such comparison.
Silent, permanent, and repaired only by resynchronizing the table by
hand.

What makes it worth reporting rather than filing under the known gap:
the other consequences of REPACK's MVCC unsafety are transient -- a
check reads an empty table for an instant and the next read is fine.
This one is not.  The copy is a single shot, and getting it wrong loses
the table's contents on the subscriber for good.

It is not about replication at all, which took another two experiments
to establish.  REPACK (CONCURRENTLY) builds the new heap by inserting
the rows as itself -- heapam_relation_copy_for_cluster() sends the
concurrent case to heap_insert_for_repack(), an ordinary heap_insert(),
where the non-concurrent case goes to reform_and_rewrite_tuple() and
preserves each tuple's xmin and xmax.  Every row of the new heap
therefore carries the repacking transaction's xid, and any snapshot
older than that commit sees the whole table as written by a
transaction it does not consider committed.

A reader that has already touched the table is safe by accident: it
holds AccessShareLock, so the swap waits for it rather than overtaking
it.  What is exposed is a holder of an old snapshot that has not yet
opened the table -- and there are three of those, measured:

  an ordinary REPEATABLE READ transaction, snapshot taken, table not
  yet read                                     0 of 10 rows, every time

  a snapshot exported from a logical slot and read under SET
  TRANSACTION SNAPSHOT, which is what pg_dump --snapshot does and what
  the documentation recommends for building a subscriber by hand
                                               0 rows, 4 rounds of 4

  logical replication's initial tablesync      0 rows, 5 rounds of 6,
                                               and permanently

The first two are deterministic.  The third needs the repack to land in
the window between the exported snapshot and the COPY, which is why a
small table reproduces it and a large one does not.

All three are src/test/subscription/t/100_repack_snapshots.pl, skipped
unless

  PG_TEST_EXTRA='repack_snapshots'

since it fails on purpose while the gap is open.  A ten-row table is
not a curiosity: the window is the gap between the slot's exported
snapshot and the COPY that reads with it -- slot creation, worker
startup, the handshake -- so a table small enough to copy instantly
spends all of its synchronization inside the window, while a large one
mostly misses it.  Four hundred thousand rows did not reproduce it in
six rounds; ten rows reproduced it in five of six.

The suite's own exposure is closed: the subscription topology now waits
for the initial synchronization to finish before the workload starts,
which it should have done from the beginning -- wait_for_catchup
follows the walsender's LSN and says nothing about tablesync, a caveat
the topology already had written down in its final and had not applied
to its setup.
